ANSIBLE

What is Ansible

Ansible is an automation tool used to manage and configure multiple servers from a single machine.

It is mainly used for

configuration management

application deployment

server automation

Example
If we want to install nginx on 10 servers, we do not need to login to each server manually. Ansible can install it on all servers with one command.

Why Ansible is used

agentless tool (no software needed on servers)

easy to learn

uses ssh for connection

saves time by automating repetitive tasks

manages many servers at the same time

How Ansible works

There are four main components

Control Node
The machine where Ansible is installed.

Managed Nodes
The servers that Ansible controls.

Inventory
A file that contains the IP addresses of servers.

Playbook
A file that contains automation tasks written in YAML.

Flow

Control Node → SSH → Managed Servers

Playbook

A playbook is a YAML file that contains tasks which Ansible runs on servers.

Examples of tasks

installing software

starting services

creating files

copying files

updating servers

YAML basics

Ansible playbooks are written in YAML.

Rules

indentation is very important

always use spaces not tabs

format is key : value

Example

name: install nginx
hosts: web
become: yes

Basic playbook structure

name: install nginx on server
hosts: web
become: yes

tasks:

name: install nginx
apt:
name: nginx
state: present

Explanation

name → description of playbook
hosts → servers where playbook will run
become → run command as root user
tasks → list of tasks to execute

How to run a playbook

Command

ansible-playbook playbook.yml

Example

ansible-playbook nginx.yml

Example playbook to test connection

name: ping servers
hosts: all

tasks:

name: test connection
ping:

Install apache example

name: install apache
hosts: web
become: yes

tasks:

name: install apache2
apt:
name: apache2
state: present

Start service example

name: start apache
hosts: web
become: yes

tasks:

name: start apache service
service:
name: apache2
state: started

Create file example

name: create file
hosts: web

tasks:

name: create test file
file:
path: /home/ubuntu/test.txt
state: touch

Check playbook syntax

ansible-playbook playbook.yml --syntax-check

Dry run (test playbook without making changes)

ansible-playbook playbook.yml --check

Common modules in ansible

ping → check connection
apt → install packages in ubuntu
yum → install packages in amazon linux / centos
service → manage services
copy → copy files to server
file → create or delete files

Copy file example

name: copy file
hosts: web

tasks:

name: copy index file
copy:
src: index.html
dest: /var/www/html/index.html

Run playbook for specific server

ansible-playbook nginx.yml --limit web1

Check hosts in inventory

ansible all --list-hosts